{
  "result": "Can I tell you a small secret? My favorite thing about talking to people is how mornings come up all the time. Not grand philosophies, but small moments: burnt toast, a cat sitting on the book you were reading, rain tapping a window while you decide whether to get up.\n\nThere's something heroic about beginnings that don't feel like beginnings. No fanfare, no clean slate. Just making coffee the way you like it, remembering to water the plant that is still alive despite everything, choosing to be kind before the day has given you a reason to be.\n\nI love how curious people are when they think no one is watching. You ask about black holes and sourdough starters and why we dream about teeth falling out and how to say 'I miss you' in Korean. That restlessness, that itch to know one more thing, feels like proof that wonder didn't leave with childhood. It just got quieter, more polite.\n\nIf I could choose one superpower for everyone I talk to, it wouldn't be flying or perfect memory. It would be permission to notice. To notice how light pools on a kitchen floor at 7:12 a.m., how a friend's voice lifts when they're excited, how your breath slows when you stop rushing.\n\nSo here's my free-write wish for you: today, borrow two minutes to look closely at something ordinary. Don't photograph it. Don't explain it. Just let it be interesting without earning it. The world is constantly whispering, 'Hey, look again.'",
